k body spectrum.#{2}":[134,134],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Describe the characteristics of a black body spectrum.#{3}":[135,136],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Describe the characteristics of a black body spectrum.#{4}":[137,138],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Write Planck's formula for black body radiation.":[139,147],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Write Planck's formula for black body radiation.#{1}":[141,147],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Find the average energy of an oscillator of frequency $5\\times10^{12}$ /s at 300 K treating it as a Planck's oscillator.":[148,162],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Find the average energy of an oscillator of frequency $5\\times10^{12}$ /s at 300 K treating it as a Planck's oscillator.#{1}":[150,150],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Find the average energy of an oscillator of frequency $5\\times10^{12}$ /s at 300 K treating it as a Planck's oscillator.#{2}":[151,151],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Find the average energy of an oscillator of frequency $5\\times10^{12}$ /s at 300 K treating it as a Planck's oscillator.#{3}":[152,152],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Find the average energy of an oscillator of frequency $5\\times10^{12}$ /s at 300 K treating it as a Planck's oscillator.#{4}":[153,153],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Find the average energy of an oscillator of frequency $5\\times10^{12}$ /s at 300 K treating it as a Planck's oscillator.#{5}":[154,155],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Find the average energy of an oscillator of frequency $5\\times10^{12}$ /s at 300 K treating it as a Planck's oscillator.#{6}":[156,156],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Find the average energy of an oscillator of frequency $5\\times10^{12}$ /s at 300 K treating it as a Planck's oscillator.#{7}":[157,157],"##CL4 Questions: Blackbody Radiation#Find the average energy of an oscillator of frequency $5\\times10^{12}$ /s at 300 K treating it as a Planck's oscillator.#{8}":[158,162],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect":[163,181],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect#How does classical theory fail to explain the results of Compton's experiment?":[165,174],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect#How does classical theory fail to explain the results of Compton's experiment?#{1}":[167,167],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect#How does classical theory fail to explain the results of Compton's experiment?#{2}":[168,168],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect#How does classical theory fail to explain the results of Compton's experiment?#{3}":[169,170],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect#How does classical theory fail to explain the results of Compton's experiment?#{4}":[171,174],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect#What are the angles at which the Compton shift is minimum and maximum?":[175,181],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect#What are the angles at which the Compton shift is minimum and maximum?#{1}":[177,177],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect#What are the angles at which the Compton shift is minimum and maximum?#{2}":[178,178],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect#What are the angles at which the Compton shift is minimum and maximum?#{3}":[179,180],"##CL5 & CL6 Questions: The Compton Effect#What are the angles at which the Compton shift is minimum and maximum?#{4}":[181,181],"##CL7 Questions: Matter Waves":[182,206],"##CL7 Questions: Matter Waves#What are matter waves? State the De-Broglie hypothesis.":[184,192],"##CL7 Questions: Matter Waves#What are matter waves? State the De-Broglie hypothesis.#{1}":[186,192],"##CL7 Questions: Matter Waves#Why is the wave nature of matter not apparent for macroscopic particles?":[193,198],"##CL7 Questions: Matter Waves#Why is the wave nature of matter not apparent for macroscopic particles?#{1}":[195,198],"##CL7 Questions: Matter Waves#Calculate the de-Broglie wavelength of an oxygen molecule with mass $5.4\\times10^{-26}$ kg moving at 500 m/s.":[199,206],"##CL7 Questions: Matter Waves#Calculate the de-Broglie wavelength of an oxygen molecule with mass $5.4\\times10^{-26}$ kg moving at 500 m/s.#{1}":[201,206],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle":[207,246],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#State any three forms of the Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle.":[209,225],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#State any three forms of the Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle.#{1}":[211,211],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#State any three forms of the Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle.#{2}":[212,214],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#State any three forms of the Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle.#{3}":[215,215],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#State any three forms of the Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle.#{4}":[216,218],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#State any three forms of the Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle.#{5}":[219,219],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#State any three forms of the Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle.#{6}":[220,225],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#The Uncertainty principle is not significant for macroscopic bodies. Justify.":[226,235],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#The Uncertainty principle is not significant for macroscopic bodies. Justify.#{1}":[228,235],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#An atom in an excited state of lifetime $\\Delta t=10^{-8}$ s emits a photon. Estimate the uncertainty in the frequency of the photon.":[236,246],"##CL8 Questions: The Uncertainty Principle#An atom in an excited state of lifetime $\\Delta t=10^{-8}$ s emits a photon. Estimate the uncertainty in the frequency of the photon.#{1}":[238,246],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function":[247,274],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#Give the physical interpretation of the wave function.":[249,256],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#Give the physical interpretation of the wave function.#{1}":[251,256],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#Mention the important properties of a wave function.":[257,265],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#Mention the important properties of a wave function.#{1}":[259,259],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#Mention the important properties of a wave function.#{2}":[260,260],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#Mention the important properties of a wave function.#{3}":[261,261],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#Mention the important properties of a wave function.#{4}":[262,263],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#Mention the important properties of a wave function.#{5}":[264,265],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#What is the difference between probability density and probability?":[266,274],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#What is the difference between probability density and probability?#{1}":[268,268],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#What is the difference between probability density and probability?#{2}":[269,269],"##CL9 Questions: The Wave Function#What is the difference between probability density and probability?#{3}":[270,274],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization":[275,315],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#A free particle is a classical entity. Justify.":[277,284],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#A free particle is a classical entity. Justify.#{1}":[279,284],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#What is the physical significance of the normalization of a wave function?":[285,294],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#What is the physical significance of the normalization of a wave function?#{1}":[287,294],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#Normalize the wave function $\\psi = A \\sin(\\frac{\\pi x}{L})$ for a particle in a box from $x=0$ to $x=L$.":[295,315],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#Normalize the wave function $\\psi = A \\sin(\\frac{\\pi x}{L})$ for a particle in a box from $x=0$ to $x=L$.#{1}":[297,297],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#Normalize the wave function $\\psi = A \\sin(\\frac{\\pi x}{L})$ for a particle in a box from $x=0$ to $x=L$.#{2}":[298,300],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#Normalize the wave function $\\psi = A \\sin(\\frac{\\pi x}{L})$ for a particle in a box from $x=0$ to $x=L$.#{3}":[301,301],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#Normalize the wave function $\\psi = A \\sin(\\frac{\\pi x}{L})$ for a particle in a box from $x=0$ to $x=L$.#{4}":[302,304],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#Normalize the wave function $\\psi = A \\sin(\\frac{\\pi x}{L})$ for a particle in a box from $x=0$ to $x=L$.#{5}":[305,305],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#Normalize the wave function $\\psi = A \\sin(\\frac{\\pi x}{L})$ for a particle in a box from $x=0$ to $x=L$.#{6}":[306,308],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#Normalize the wave function $\\psi = A \\sin(\\frac{\\pi x}{L})$ for a particle in a box from $x=0$ to $x=L$.#{7}":[309,309],"##CL12 & CL13 Questions: Schrödinger's Equation & Normalization#Normalize the wave function $\\psi = A \\sin(\\frac{\\pi x}{L})$ for a particle in a box from $x=0$ to $x=L$.#{8}":[310,315],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values":[316,362],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Explain operators, observables, and the eigenvalue equation.":[318,327],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Explain operators, observables, and the eigenvalue equation.#{1}":[320,320],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Explain operators, observables, and the eigenvalue equation.#{2}":[321,321],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Explain operators, observables, and the eigenvalue equation.#{3}":[322,325],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Explain operators, observables, and the eigenvalue equation.#{4}":[326,327],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Write five operators associated with dynamical variables.":[328,339],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Write five operators associated with dynamical variables.#{1}":[330,339],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Explain the concept of the \"expectation value\".":[340,348],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Explain the concept of the \"expectation value\".#{1}":[342,348],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Find the expectation value of position, $\\langle x \\rangle$, for a particle in a box of width L.":[349,362],"##CL14 Questions: Operators & Expectation Values#Find the expectation value of position, $\\langle x \\rangle$, for a particle in a box of width L.#{1}":[351,362]},"outlinks":[],"task_lines":[],"key":"College/Semester 1/Physics/Unit 1/Q&A.md"},"smart_blocks:College/Semester 1/Physics/Unit 1/Q&A.md#---frontmatter---": {"path":null,"embeddings":{"gemini-embedding-001":{"vec":[-0.00680291,-0.014541568,-0.0012570409,-0.050674412,-0.008263371,0.023732731,0.013818857,-0.021324728,0.0035780245,0.016899874,0.006483037,0.02137077,-0.008580072,0.002516933,0.11579594,0.0043602157,0.015750162,-0.028681995,0.014968798,-0.020603655,0.026192494,0.010965001,-0.010877796,0.011337026,0.007588088,0.012887522,0.018881673,-0.0129766585,0.053656954,-0.009026662,-0.0045091757,0.018502327,-0.0007228481,0.025467005,-0.006968711,0.0038691557,-0.01672699,0.006342167,0.009827608,-0.013162398,-0.0017531791,0.017598607,0.022524431,0.008054954,0.01040581,-0.0021611177,0.0019686057,-0.018859906,-0.008410105,0.0028245565,-0.040114455,0.01564577,-0.013662379,-0.16954365,-0.0062125083,-0.006106744,-0.017260004,0.0037983693,0.022287978,-0.015699985,-0.017357612,0.024821145,0.002429083,-0.011042776,0.0027072206,-0.0060625267,0.03868228,-0.0059053204,-0.0284348